# zenjpeg NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench -p zenjpeg --bench tier_isolation --features _dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
input:   1024x1024 noise+patches (per this repo's banned-test-images rule: no
         gradients, no Kodak), Q85 4:2:0

## Why this file exists

zenjpeg has ~139 SIMD dispatch sites and ~20 files carrying NEON kernels, and
no bench could tell you what any of them were worth on ARM. The existing SIMD
benches either compare zenjpeg against mozjpeg/libjpeg-turbo — which answers
"are we competitive", not "is our SIMD earning its keep" — or are written
`#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]` and do not build on aarch64 at all
(`mage_simd.rs` is entirely x86-gated). A kernel slower than its own scalar
fallback was invisible to both.

## Result

| pipeline | NEON    | scalar  | speedup |
|----------|---------|---------|---------|
| encode   | 25.6ms  | 32.7ms  |  1.28x  |
| decode   | 13.2ms  | 17.3ms  |  1.31x  |

No regression.

## Context: this is on the low side, and that is expected here

Compare the same method on the same host: zenflate checksums 12-21x, zenpng
scan predicates 3.6-7.7x, butteraugli 3.4-3.7x, zenresize linear 4.0-4.3x, vs
zenjpeg 1.3x. JPEG encode/decode is not one big vectorisable kernel — a large
share of both pipelines is Huffman/entropy coding, which is inherently serial
(this repo's own profiling notes call entropy encoding "inherently serial" and
put it at ~11.7% of encode, with AQ 14.5%, DCT+quant 8.6%, deringing 6.9%).
1.3x end-to-end is consistent with SIMD covering the transform/colour/AQ
stages while the entropy stages stay scalar by nature.

NOT MEASURED here: the per-stage split on ARM. The percentages above are from
this repo's existing x86 flamegraph notes, not from this run. Attributing the
remaining time on aarch64 needs a profile.
